"A universality theorem for stressable graphs in the plane (https://arxiv.org/html/1902.07212v4)\nciteturn1341view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1340view1\",\"pattern\":\"Lemma 4\"}); Total lines: 306\nL206: Assume that $\\mathfrak{s}$ is a stress of $(\\Gamma,p)$. By Proposition 【32†1】, $\\mathfrak{s}=\\lambda\\mathfrak{a}+\\sum\\lambda_{i}\\mathfrak{d}_{i}$ for some real coefficients. Consider the stress $\\mathfrak{s}^{\\prime}=\\lambda\\mathfrak{a}^{\\prime}+\\sum\\lambda_{i}\\mathfrak{d}^{\\prime}_{i}$ of $(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$. By Lemma 【31†2】 (4) and Lemma 【33†3】, we have $SIGN(\\mathfrak{s})=SIGN(\\mathfrak{s}^{\\prime})$.\nL207: Conversely, each stress $\\mathfrak{s}^{\\prime}$ of $(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$, has a similar counterpart for $(\\Gamma,p)$.∎\nL208: ###### Proposition 3.\nL209: \nL210: $\\pi$ is a stable projection.\nL211: \nL212: Proof. Assume that a line arrangement $L^{\\prime}$ belongs to the realization space $\\mathcal{R}(L)$. Let us look at the preimage $\\pi^{-1}(L^{\\prime})$. Specification of $A^{\\prime}B^{\\prime}C^{\\prime}D^{\\prime}$ is stable since the positions of the lines $A^{\\prime}B^{\\prime},\\ A^{\\prime}D^{\\prime}$, etc. are defined by a number of strict inequalities depending on the lines $L$.\nL213: Now we may choose arbitrary distinct points $A_{1}^{\\prime},...,A^{\\prime}_{n}$ on the segment $A^{\\prime}B^{\\prime}$ that come in the same order as $A_{1},A_{2},...,A_{n}$. The same happens with $B_{1}^{\\prime},...,B^{\\prime}_{n}$: here we care about their order only. Now let us choose $C^{\\prime}_{1}$ as an arbitrary point on the segment $B^{\\prime}C^{\\prime}$.\nL214: Once $C^{\\prime}$ is specified, the position of $D_{1}^{\\prime}$ is uniquely determined, since Desargues condition implies that the lines $A^{\\prime}C^{\\prime}$, $A^{\\prime}_{1}B^{\\prime}_{1}$, and $C^{\\prime}_{1}D^{\\prime}_{1}$ meet at a point.\nL215: The point $C_{2}^{\\prime}$ should be chosen to the left of $C_{1}^{\\prime}$ but in such a way that $D_{2}^{\\prime}$ lies to the right of $D^{\\prime}_{1}$. This is always possible but dictates some extra condition, still in the framework of stable equivalence. The rest of the points $C^{\\prime}_{i}$ and $D^{\\prime}_{i}$ are treated analogously. ∎\nL216: \nL217: Corollary 【34†1】 and Proposition 【35†3】 imply that $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ is stably equivalent to $\\mathfrak{A}$. Theorem 1 is proven.\nL218: ## 3. Relations between different settings. Proof of Theorem 【36†2】.\nL219: \nL220: In the section we show the equivalence of the settings of the present paper and that of [【15†3】].\nL221: \nL222: Let us start with the definition of equilibrium stress. The paper [【15†3】] puts no restrictions on a realization of a graph $p$, that is, the endpoints of an edge might be mapped to one and the same point. Besides, [【15†3】] presents a more usual setting of the equilibrium stress (as in [【37†2】]): the equilibrium condition reads as\nL223:  | $$\\sum_{(ij)\\in E}{s}(i,j)(p_{i}-p_{j})=0.$$  |\nL224: \nL225: Let us denote by $S(\\Gamma,p)$ the linear space of stresses and by $M(\\Gamma,p)=SIGN(S(\\Gamma,p))$ the associated matroid.\nL226: \nL227: Clearly, if no edge is degenerate (that is, $p_{i}-p_{j}\\neq 0$), a stress $s$ in this setting gives a stress in the setting of the present paper $\\mathfrak{s}(i,j)=s(i,j)|p_{i}-p_{j}|$ and vice versa. Therefore, ${M}(\\Gamma,p)=\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)$. The only subtlety may arise if a realization $p$ produces degenerate edges.\nL228: ###### Lemma 4.\nL229: \nL230: The matroid ${M}(\\Gamma,p)$ ”knows” all the degenerate edges. In particular, if there exists $p\\in R(\\Gamma,M)$ with no degenerate edges, then each $p^{\\prime}\\in R(\\Gamma,M)$ has no degenerate edges.\nL231: \nL232: Proof. Degenerate edges are detected by almost everywhere zero stresses: an edge number $i$ is degenerate for $(\\Gamma,p)$ iff $({0,...,0,+,0,0,...,0})\\in M(\\Gamma,p)$.∎\nL233: ### Strong equivalence vs weak equivalence\nL234: \nL235: Assume that a realization $p$ of a graph $\\Gamma$ has no degenerate edges.\nL236: \nL237: Repeating [【17†6】], let us say that two realizations of one and the same graph $(\\Gamma,p)$ and $(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$ are strongly equivalent, if there exists a sign preserving homeomorphism between the stress spaces $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p)$ and $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$.\nL238: Two realizations of one and the same graph $(\\Gamma,p)$ and $(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$ are weakly equivalent, if the associated matroids coincide: $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)=\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$.\nL239: \nL240: Classes of weak equivalence are realization spaces, defined in the Introduction. Classes of strong equivalence are strata considered in [【17†6】].^{4}^{4} 4 To be more precise, in [【17†6】] the strata are the connected components of the classes of strong equivalence.\nL241: ###### Proposition 4.\nL242: \nL243: Strong equivalence equals weak equivalence.\nL244: Proof. Clearly, strong equivalence implies weak equivalence. Let us prove the converse. The linear space $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p)$ is tiled by convex cones, each cone corresponds to some string of signs from $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)$. Let us intersect this tiling with the unit sphere centered at the origin. This gives a tiling of the sphere where each tile is a spherically convex polytope.\nL245: The matroid $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)$ ”knows” the incidence relation of the tiles: a tile labeled by $(\\varepsilon_{1},...,\\varepsilon_{e})$, $\\varepsilon_{i}\\in\\{+,-,0\\}$ belongs to the closure of the tile $(\\varepsilon^{\\prime}_{1},...,\\varepsilon^{\\prime}_{e})$ iff either $\\varepsilon_{i}=0$, or $\\varepsilon_{i}=\\varepsilon_{i}^{\\prime}$.\nL246: Besides, the matroid $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)$ ”knows” the dimension of each tile. Indeed, the matroid records the face poset of each tile. Since each tile is some pointed cone, its dimension is determined by the length of a longest chain in the poset.\nL247: Now it becomes possible to inductively build a sign-preserving homeomorphism between two spaces $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p)$ and $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$ with equal matroids. One should start with zero-dimensional spherical tiles, then extend the homeomorphism to one-dimensional tiles, etc. ∎\nL248: Now let us prove Theorem 【36†2】. Given $\\mathfrak{A}$, take the pair $(\\Gamma,p)$ as in the proof in Theorem 【18†1】. By Lemma 【38†4】, $R(\\Gamma,p)=\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,p)$. By Proposition 【39†4】, $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,p)$ is a strong equivalence class, that is, a stratum in the sense of [【15†3】], [【16†5】], [【17†6】]. Finally, by Theorem 【18†1】 the stratum is stably equivalent to $\\mathfrak{A}$.\nL249: ## 4. Appendix\nL250: \nL251: ### One more example\nL252: \nL253: A simpler (but in a sense, more ”degenerate”) example of $(\\Gamma^{\\prime},p)$ with the same realization space as in the previous section can be obtained if one takes $(\\Gamma,p)$ from Fig. 【21†1】, removes all the edges lying on $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, $DA$, $AC$, $BD$ $A_{i}B_{i}$, $B_{i}C_{i}$, $C_{i}D_{i}$, and adds the new edges $A_{i}D_{i}$. That is, all the edges of the new graph lie on the lines $l_{i}$.\nL254: ### ”No parallel edges” condition\nL255: \nL256: The graph from Figure 【21†1】 has parallel edges emanating from one and the same vertex (in fact, almost all the vertices have parallel emanating edges). If parallel edges emanating of one and the same vertex are forbidden we still have a universality-type theorem:\nL257: ###### Theorem 3.\nL258: \nL259: For each OBP semialgebraic set $\\mathfrak{A}$, there exists a graph $\\Gamma$, an oriented matroid $\\mathcal{M}$, and a number $N$ such that\nL260: \nL261:   1. (1)\nL262: \nL263: $(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ has a realization with no parallel edges at all, and\nL264: \nL265:   2. (2)\nL266: \nL267: the realization space $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ is stably equivalent to $2^{N}$ disjoint copies of $\\mathfrak{A}$.\nL268: Proof. The idea is depicted in Fig. 【40†5】: take the graph from Figure 【21†1】 and for each edge $(ij)$, add two new vertices and replace $(ij)$ by five new edges. One imagines a stressed realization of $K_{4}$ added to a stressed realization of $\\Gamma$ in such a way that the stresses on $(ij)$ cancel. Denote the realization of the new graph by $(\\hat{\\Gamma},\\hat{p})$, and set $\\hat{M}=M(\\hat{\\Gamma},\\hat{p})$. There exists a natural mapping\nL269: \nL270:  | $$R(\\hat{\\Gamma},\\hat{M})\\rightarrow R({\\Gamma},{M}).$$  |\nL271: The preimage of each point has $2^{e(\\Gamma)}$ connected components since each stressed copy of $K_{4}$ can be attached both on the righthand side and on the lefthand side of $(ij)$, but never degenerates. ∎\nL272: \nL273: 【41†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 5. Adding a stressed copy of $K_{4}$.\nL274: ### 4.1. Intersection of closures of two strata is not necessarily the closure of a stratum\nL275: \nL276: This phenomenon was observed in [【14†4】] for Grassmanian stratifications. Let us adjust an example from [【14†4】] to show the same for stressed graphs.\nL277: Take the point configuration from Fig. 【42†6】 and associate to it a graph $(\\Gamma,p)$ by the following rule: for each three collinear points $i,j,k$ add the edges $(ij)$, $(jk)$, and $(ik)$. So each three collinear points yield a stressable subgraph $K_{3}$. We conclude that all the collinearities of vertices persist for all the elements of the realization space $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p))$.\nL278: However, all these collinearities imply that the four points $1,2,3,4$ are harmonic, that is, their cross ratio equals $-1$.\nL279: Let us take a realization $p^{\\prime}$ of $\\Gamma$ with all the vertices lying on a line. The corresponding matroid depends on the order of the vertices only and ”does not see” the cross ratio. Therefore the intersection of the closures of the strata $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p))$ and $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime}))$ is not a closure of a stratum.\nL280: \nL281: 【43†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 6. A graph which forces harmonic relation.\nL282: ## References\nL283:   * [1] A. Björner, M. Las Vergnas, B. Sturmfels, N. White, and G. M. Ziegler, Oriented matroids. volume 46 of Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Applications. Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, second edition, 1999.\nL284:   * [2] R. Connelly, Rigidity. In: Gruber, P.M., Wills, J.M. (eds.) Handbook of Convex Geometry, vol. A, pp. 223—271. North-Holland, Amsterdam (1993)\nL285:   * [3] F. Doray, O. Karpenkov, J. Schepers, Geometry of configuration spaces of tensegrities. Discrete Comput. Geom. (2010), 43(2), 436—466.\nL286:   * [4] I. Gelfand, M. Goresky, R. MacPherson, and V. Serganova, Combinatorial geometries, convex polyhedra, and Schubert cells. Adv. Math. 63 (1987), 301—316.\nL287:   * [5] O. Karpenkov, Open Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities. Arnold Math J. (2018) 4, 1, 19—25.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA universality theorem for stressable graphs in the plane (https://arxiv.org/html/1902.07212v4)\nciteturn1341view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1340view1\",\"pattern\":\"4.1\"}); Total lines: 306\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: 【0†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org】 Report Issue 【1†Back to Abstract】 【2†Download PDF】 Contents Reading mode Colour theme\nL13:   1. 【3†Abstract.】\nL14:   2. 【4†1 Preliminaries and the main theorem】\nL15:   3. 【5†2 Proof of Theorem】\nL16:   4. 【6†3 Relations between different settings. Proof of Theorem .】\nL17:   5. 【7†4 Appendix】\nL18:     1. 【8†4.1 Intersection of closures of two strata is not necessarily the closure of a stratum】\nL19:   6. 【9†References】\nL20: \nL21: 【10†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL22: \nL23: arXiv:1902.07212v4 [math.CO] 29 Oct 2019\nL24: # A universality theorem for stressable graphs in the plane\nL25: \nL26: Gaiane Panina Address: G. Panina: St. Petersburg Department of Steklov Mathematical Institute, St. Petersburg State University, gaiane-panina@rambler.ru\nL27: ###### Abstract.\nL28: \nL29: Universality theorems (in the sense of N. Mnëv) claim that the realization space of a combinatorial object (a point configuration, a hyperplane arrangement, a convex polytope, etc.) can be arbitrarily complicated. In the paper, we prove a universality theorem for a graph in the plane with a prescribed oriented matroid of stresses, that is the collection of signs of all possible equilibrium stresses of the graph.\nL30: This research is motivated by the Grassmanian stratification (Gelfand, Goresky, MacPherson, Serganova) by thin Schubert cells, and by a recent series of papers on stratifications of configuration spaces of tensegrities (Doray, Karpenkov, Schepers, Servatius).\nL31: ###### Key words and phrases:\nL32: \nL33: Maxwell-Cremona correspondence, Grassmanian stratification, oriented matroid, equilibrium stress\nL34: ## 1. Preliminaries and the main theorem\nL35: \nL36: Let $\\Gamma=(V,E)$ be a graph without loops and multiple edges, where $V=\\{v_{1},...,v_{m}\\}$ is the set of vertices, and $E$ is the set of edges. A realization of $\\Gamma$ is a map $p:V\\rightarrow\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ such that $(ij)\\in E$ implies $p(v_{i})\\neq p(v_{j})$. We abbreviate $p(v_{i})$ as $p_{i}$.\nL37: That is, we have a planar drawing of $\\Gamma$ with possible intersections of edges and possible coinciding vertices. However, each edge is mapped to a non-degenerate line segment.\nL38: \nL39: A stress $\\mathfrak{s}$ on a realization $(\\Gamma,p)$ is an assignment of real scalars $\\mathfrak{s}(i,j)$ to the edges. One imagines that each edge is represented by a (either compressed or extended) spring. Each spring produces some forces at its endpoints.\nL40: A stress $\\mathfrak{s}$ is called a self-stress, or an equilibrium stress, if at every vertex $p_{i}$, the sum of the forces produced by the springs vanishes:\nL41: \nL42:  | $$\\sum_{(ij)\\in E}\\mathfrak{s}(i,j)\\mathbf{u}_{ij}=0.$$  |\nL43: \nL44: Here $\\mathbf{u}_{ij}=\\frac{p_{i}-p_{j}}{|p_{i}-p_{j}|}$ is the unit vector pointing from $p_{j}$ to $p_{i}$.\nL45: \nL46: A self-stress is non-trivial if it is not identically zero.\nL47: The set of all self-stresses $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p)$ is a linear space which naturally embeds in $\\mathbb{R}^{e}$, where $e=|E|$; the space $\\mathfrak{S}$ depends on $p$.\nL48: \nL49: A realization $(\\Gamma,p)$ is stressable if $dim~\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p)>0.$\nL50: \nL51: Given $(\\Gamma,p)$, define an oriented matroid $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p):=SIGN(\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p))$.\nL52: In simple words, to obtain the matroid, enumerate somehow the edges of the graph, and for each non-trivial stress, list the signs of $\\mathfrak{s}(i,j)$. We obtain a collection of strings (elements of $(+,-,0)^{\\sharp(E)}$), which is an oriented matroid^{1}^{1} 1 This is some realizable oriented matroid indeed, since it represents the set of vectors of some easy-to-build vector configuration related to the rigidity matrix. For rigidity matrices see [【11†10】]..\nL53: For the purpose of the present paper, it is sufficient to imagine an oriented matroid as a collection of strings. For a general theory of oriented matroids see [【12†1】].\nL54: Example: let $(\\Gamma,p)$ be a planar realization of the graph $K_{4}$ such that $p_{4}$ lies inside the triangle $p_{1}p_{2}p_{3}$. Assume that the edges are enumerated in a way such that first come the edges of the triangle. Then $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)=\\{(+++---),(---+++)\\}$.\nL55: \nL56: The realization space of a graph $\\Gamma$ is the space of all realizations of $\\Gamma$ factorized by the action of the affine group:\nL57: \nL58:  | $$\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma)=\\{p:p\\hbox{ is a realization of }\\Gamma\\}/Aff(\\mathbb{R}^{2}).$$  |\nL59: Given a graph $\\Gamma$ and an oriented matroid $\\mathcal{M}$, the realization space of $(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ is the space of all realizations of $\\Gamma$ that yield the oriented matroid $\\mathcal{M}$:\nL60: \nL61:  | $$\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})=\\{p\\in\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma):\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)=\\mathcal{M}\\}.$$  |\nL62: \nL63: For a fixed graph $\\Gamma$, the realization spaces $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ stratify $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma)$. Each of $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ becomes a stratum.\nL64: In general, semialgebraic sets are subsets of some Euclidean space $\\mathbb{R}^{N}$ defined by polynomial equations and inequalities. A semialgebraic set is called a open basic primary semialgebraic set (OBP semialgebraic set) if there are no defining equations, all the defining inequalities are strict, and the coefficients of all the defining polynomials are rational.\nL65: We borrow the notion of stable equivalency from traditional papers on universality, e.g. from [【13†8】]: stable equivalence is an equivalence relation on OBP semialgebraic sets generated by rational equivalence and stable projections.\nL66: \nL67: The main result of the paper is:\nL68: ###### Theorem 1.\nL69: \nL70: For each open basic primary semialgebraic set $\\mathfrak{A}$, there exists a graph $\\Gamma$ and an oriented matroid $\\mathcal{M}$ such that the realization space $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ is stably equivalent to $\\mathfrak{A}$.\nL71: Our first motivation comes from the complex Grassmanian stratifications [【14†4】], where strata are labeled by realizable matroids, and each stratum equals the realization space of a matroid. The stratification has a version over the field $\\mathbb{R}$, where strata are labeled by realizable oriented matroids.\nL72: The other motivation is a series of papers [【15†3】], [【16†5】], [【17†6】] on stratifications of configuration spaces of tensegrities. Although the setup of the present paper might look different from the setup of [【15†3】], [【16†5】], [【17†6】], there is very much in common, see Section 3. In particular, we have the following analogue of Theorem 【18†1】:\nL73: ###### Theorem 2.\nL74: \nL75: For each connected open basic primary semialgebraic set $\\mathfrak{A}$, there exists a graph $\\Gamma$ and a stratum (in the sense of [【15†3】]) $R$ in the realization space of $\\Gamma$ such that $R$ is stably equivalent to $\\mathfrak{A}$.\nL76: \nL77: Acknowledgement. The author is grateful to Joseph Gordon and Yana Teplitskaya for multiple discussions. This research is supported by the Russian Science Foundation under grant 16-11-10039.\nL245: The matroid $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)$ ”knows” the incidence relation of the tiles: a tile labeled by $(\\varepsilon_{1},...,\\varepsilon_{e})$, $\\varepsilon_{i}\\in\\{+,-,0\\}$ belongs to the closure of the tile $(\\varepsilon^{\\prime}_{1},...,\\varepsilon^{\\prime}_{e})$ iff either $\\varepsilon_{i}=0$, or $\\varepsilon_{i}=\\varepsilon_{i}^{\\prime}$.\nL246: Besides, the matroid $\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p)$ ”knows” the dimension of each tile. Indeed, the matroid records the face poset of each tile. Since each tile is some pointed cone, its dimension is determined by the length of a longest chain in the poset.\nL247: Now it becomes possible to inductively build a sign-preserving homeomorphism between two spaces $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p)$ and $\\mathfrak{S}(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime})$ with equal matroids. One should start with zero-dimensional spherical tiles, then extend the homeomorphism to one-dimensional tiles, etc. ∎\nL248: Now let us prove Theorem 【36†2】. Given $\\mathfrak{A}$, take the pair $(\\Gamma,p)$ as in the proof in Theorem 【18†1】. By Lemma 【38†4】, $R(\\Gamma,p)=\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,p)$. By Proposition 【39†4】, $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,p)$ is a strong equivalence class, that is, a stratum in the sense of [【15†3】], [【16†5】], [【17†6】]. Finally, by Theorem 【18†1】 the stratum is stably equivalent to $\\mathfrak{A}$.\nL249: ## 4. Appendix\nL250: \nL251: ### One more example\nL252: \nL253: A simpler (but in a sense, more ”degenerate”) example of $(\\Gamma^{\\prime},p)$ with the same realization space as in the previous section can be obtained if one takes $(\\Gamma,p)$ from Fig. 【21†1】, removes all the edges lying on $AB$, $BC$, $CD$, $DA$, $AC$, $BD$ $A_{i}B_{i}$, $B_{i}C_{i}$, $C_{i}D_{i}$, and adds the new edges $A_{i}D_{i}$. That is, all the edges of the new graph lie on the lines $l_{i}$.\nL254: ### ”No parallel edges” condition\nL255: \nL256: The graph from Figure 【21†1】 has parallel edges emanating from one and the same vertex (in fact, almost all the vertices have parallel emanating edges). If parallel edges emanating of one and the same vertex are forbidden we still have a universality-type theorem:\nL257: ###### Theorem 3.\nL258: \nL259: For each OBP semialgebraic set $\\mathfrak{A}$, there exists a graph $\\Gamma$, an oriented matroid $\\mathcal{M}$, and a number $N$ such that\nL260: \nL261:   1. (1)\nL262: \nL263: $(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ has a realization with no parallel edges at all, and\nL264: \nL265:   2. (2)\nL266: \nL267: the realization space $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M})$ is stably equivalent to $2^{N}$ disjoint copies of $\\mathfrak{A}$.\nL268: Proof. The idea is depicted in Fig. 【40†5】: take the graph from Figure 【21†1】 and for each edge $(ij)$, add two new vertices and replace $(ij)$ by five new edges. One imagines a stressed realization of $K_{4}$ added to a stressed realization of $\\Gamma$ in such a way that the stresses on $(ij)$ cancel. Denote the realization of the new graph by $(\\hat{\\Gamma},\\hat{p})$, and set $\\hat{M}=M(\\hat{\\Gamma},\\hat{p})$. There exists a natural mapping\nL269: \nL270:  | $$R(\\hat{\\Gamma},\\hat{M})\\rightarrow R({\\Gamma},{M}).$$  |\nL271: The preimage of each point has $2^{e(\\Gamma)}$ connected components since each stressed copy of $K_{4}$ can be attached both on the righthand side and on the lefthand side of $(ij)$, but never degenerates. ∎\nL272: \nL273: 【41†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 5. Adding a stressed copy of $K_{4}$.\nL274: ### 4.1. Intersection of closures of two strata is not necessarily the closure of a stratum\nL275: \nL276: This phenomenon was observed in [【14†4】] for Grassmanian stratifications. Let us adjust an example from [【14†4】] to show the same for stressed graphs.\nL277: Take the point configuration from Fig. 【42†6】 and associate to it a graph $(\\Gamma,p)$ by the following rule: for each three collinear points $i,j,k$ add the edges $(ij)$, $(jk)$, and $(ik)$. So each three collinear points yield a stressable subgraph $K_{3}$. We conclude that all the collinearities of vertices persist for all the elements of the realization space $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p))$.\nL278: However, all these collinearities imply that the four points $1,2,3,4$ are harmonic, that is, their cross ratio equals $-1$.\nL279: Let us take a realization $p^{\\prime}$ of $\\Gamma$ with all the vertices lying on a line. The corresponding matroid depends on the order of the vertices only and ”does not see” the cross ratio. Therefore the intersection of the closures of the strata $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p))$ and $\\mathcal{R}(\\Gamma,\\mathcal{M}(\\Gamma,p^{\\prime}))$ is not a closure of a stratum.\nL280: \nL281: 【43†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 6. A graph which forces harmonic relation.\nL282: ## References\nL283:   * [1] A. Björner, M. Las Vergnas, B. Sturmfels, N. White, and G. M. Ziegler, Oriented matroids. volume 46 of Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Applications. Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, second edition, 1999.\nL284:   * [2] R. Connelly, Rigidity. In: Gruber, P.M., Wills, J.M. (eds.) Handbook of Convex Geometry, vol. A, pp. 223—271. North-Holland, Amsterdam (1993)\nL285:   * [3] F. Doray, O. Karpenkov, J. Schepers, Geometry of configuration spaces of tensegrities. Discrete Comput. Geom. (2010), 43(2), 436—466.\nL286:   * [4] I. Gelfand, M. Goresky, R. MacPherson, and V. Serganova, Combinatorial geometries, convex polyhedra, and Schubert cells. Adv. Math. 63 (1987), 301—316.\nL287:   * [5] O. Karpenkov, Open Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities. Arnold Math J. (2018) 4, 1, 19—25.\nL288:   * [6] O. Karpenkov, J. Schepers, B. Servatius, On stratifications for planar tensegrities with a small number of vertices. ARS Math Contemp (2013), 6(2), 305—322.\nL289:   * [7] N. Mnëv, The topology of configuration varieties and convex polytopes varieties, PHD thesis, Leningrad, 1986 (in Russian)\nL290:   * [8] J. Richter-Gebert, Realization Spaces of Polytopes. Berlin/Heidelberg, Springer, 1996.\nL291:   * [9] J. Richter-Gebert, Mnëv’s universality theorem revisited. Séminaire Lotharingien de Combinatoire, 34, 1995.\nL292:   * [10] W. Whiteley, Matroids and rigid structures, Matroid Applications, Encyclopedia of Mathematics and its Applications, 40, Cambridge: Cambridge Univ. Press, 1992, 1 – 53.\nL293: Experimental support, please 【44†view the build logs】 for errors. Generated by 【45†L A T E xml†math.nist.gov】 .\nL294: ## Instructions for reporting errors\nL295: \nL296: We are continuing to improve HTML versions of papers, and your feedback helps enhance accessibility and mobile support. To report errors in the HTML that will help us improve conversion and rendering, choose any of the methods listed below:\nL297: \nL298:   * Click the \"Report Issue\" () button, located in the page header.\nL299: \nL300: Tip: You can select the relevant text first, to include it in your report.\nL301: Our team has already identified 【46†the following issues†github.com】. We appreciate your time reviewing and reporting rendering errors we may not have found yet. Your efforts will help us imp